7592186119994 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119994 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120629 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119994 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120703 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119994 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119994 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120742 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119994 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120743 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119994 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119994 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119994 #edb/ref 1045 "Row and Column Operations on Determinants" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119994 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186120625 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119994 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119994 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "faea29b6-21e9-5455-abc1-ad6fe37387f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120431 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409687 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120431 #edb/ref 1069 "q-42503" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120431 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120431 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $det(A)=12.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}$ and ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{3}.$ What is the value of $det(B)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120431 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0a9d752b-69e4-5f21-888f-3429a8443f7b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120431 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}$ involves swapping two rows, so it changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{3}$ involves adding a multiple of one row to another row,  so it does not change the determinant.\n\nTherefore, we only have to change the sign of the determinant to the opposite one. Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =-det(A) \\\\  & =-(12) \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120625 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120703 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120625 #edb/ref 1061 16213 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120625 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "57d74627-70e0-5830-ba95-6cb026f4de4b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120625 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121020 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120626 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120626 #edb/ref 1061 16214 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120626 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "07d38f85-837d-577c-a3fa-ed4adb824e28" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120626 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120627 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120743 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120627 #edb/ref 1061 16215 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120627 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b2bed0ab-d12e-5d90-bbf5-94e480bceb4c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120627 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119776 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120628 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120742 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120628 #edb/ref 1061 16216 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120628 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9c7f038a-e114-5e98-bc36-49762d4031f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120628 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120629 #edb/ref 1061 16217 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120629 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d5da1bd5-0cc1-56fe-a8d7-36aa6576326a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120629 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121021 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120703 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120626 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120703 #edb/ref 1061 17635 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120703 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1889d210-a2bc-5893-b7e3-879d8fc8961f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120703 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120704 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120627 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120704 #edb/ref 1061 17658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120704 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0cc1f27c-f5d8-5b74-9602-32ce4d87b799" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120704 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120742 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120629 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120742 #edb/ref 1061 22905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120742 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e1ec39b0-859a-5c8a-a8ca-f8a0f6371e76" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120742 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120743 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120743 #edb/ref 1061 22922 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120743 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ebb7b384-6a1f-56e3-9927-8911a4ec3563" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120743 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121061 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120744 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120744 #edb/ref 1061 22923 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120744 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6bb5981c-60d1-56ed-a146-3ef4d9c0f6bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120744 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121020 #edb/ref 1064 "Remember the following three standard elementary row operations:\n\nI. Swapping two rows\n\nII. Multiplying a row by a non-zero number\n\nIII. Adding a multiple of one row to another row\n\nOf these three elementary row operations, only III (adding a multiple of one row to another row) *preserves* the determinant. The other two operations, switching two rows or multiplying a row by a non-zero number, *change* the determinant.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the matrix $A = [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}].$ Taking the determinant, we have $$\\left| \\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned} \\right| = 1 \\cdot 4 - 2 \\cdot 3 = -2.$$\n\nIf we swap the two rows, we get a different determinant: $$\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =3\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 4 \\\\ & =2 \\\\ & \\ne -2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}$$\n\nLikewise, if we multiply top row by ${\\color{red}2},$ then we get a different determinant: $$\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot 1 & 2\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\ & =2\\cdot 4-3\\cdot 4 \\\\ & =-4 \\\\ & \\ne -2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}$$\n\nHowever, if we add twice the top row to the bottom row, then we get the same determinant: $$\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3+2\\cdot 1 & 4+2\\cdot 2\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 8\\end{aligned}| \\\\ & =1\\cdot 8-2\\cdot 5 \\\\ & =-2\\,✓\\end{aligned}$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121020 #edb/ref 1065 3004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121020 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e5ae8e63-4d2c-5491-9908-96fe94243ab9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121020 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121021 #edb/ref 1064 "Since $\\det(A^T)=\\det(A),$ the corresponding elementary column operations on determinants have the same properties as the elementary row ones.\n\n- Swapping two columns changes the sign of the determinant.\n\n- Multiplying a column by a number scales the determinant by that number.\n\n- Adding a multiple of one column to another column does not change the determinant." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121021 #edb/ref 1065 3005 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121021 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "bac0b72b-b76a-5a06-9312-d2b7b8ac4247" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121021 #edb/ref 1067 "Column Operations on Determinants" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121044 #edb/ref 1064 "As we saw, swapping two rows does not preserve the determinant. However, the only difference is the sign of the determinant changes. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| & =1\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 3=-2 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =3\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 4=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in the above example, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| & =-|\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, swapping any two rows of a matrix changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one. That is to say, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det[\\begin{aligned}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ ◻ & ◻ & … & ◻ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ △ & △ & … & △ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{aligned}]=-det[\\begin{aligned}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ △ & △ & … & △ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ ◻ & ◻ & … & ◻ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121044 #edb/ref 1065 3236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121044 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f151b612-1bdb-5723-af50-a48439141fbc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121044 #edb/ref 1067 "Switching Two Rows Changes the Sign of the Determinant" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121045 #edb/ref 1064 "As we have seen, multiplying a row by a number does not preserve the determinant. However, it does affect the determinant in a predictable way: multiplying a row by a number scales the determinant by that number as well.\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| & =1\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 3=-2 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot 1 & 2\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}|=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| & =2\\cdot 4-3\\cdot 4=-4\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, in the above example, we have $$\\left| \\begin{aligned}2\\cdot 1 & 2\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned} \\right| = {\\color{red}2} \\cdot \\left| \\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned} \\right|.$$\n\nIn general, multiplying a row of a matrix by a number multiplies the determinant by that number. That is to say, we can factor out the common multiplier from the row of the determinant: $$\\begin{aligned}det[\\begin{aligned}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ k△ & k△ & … & k△ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{aligned}]=k\\cdot det[\\begin{aligned}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ △ & △ & … & △ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121045 #edb/ref 1065 3237 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121045 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "09c28da6-5340-56f9-bbf5-427a824977ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121045 #edb/ref 1067 "Multiplying a Row By a Number Scales the Determinant By That Number" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121061 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's check what happens if we multiply the entire matrix (not only one row) by a number. For example,\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| & =1\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 3=-2 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot 1 & 3\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3\\cdot 3 & 3\\cdot 4\\end{aligned}|=|\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 9 & 12\\end{aligned}| & =3\\cdot 12-6\\cdot 9=-18\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, in the above example, we have\n\n$$\\left| \\begin{aligned}3\\cdot 1 & 3\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3\\cdot 3 & 3\\cdot 4\\end{aligned} \\right| = {\\color{red}3}^2 \\cdot \\left| \\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned} \\right|.$$\n\nThe above is equivalent to multiplying both rows of the matrix by $3$. Multiplying the first row scales the determinant by $3$, and then multiplying the second row scales it by $3$ once more.\n\nIn general, multiplying an entire $n \\times n$ matrix by a number multiplies the determinant by that number to the power of $n$. That is to say, we can factor out the common multiplier from the entire matrix, as follows: $$\\begin{aligned}det(k\\cdot A)={k}^{n}\\cdot det(A),\\end{aligned}$$ where $A$ is an $n \\times n$ matrix." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121061 #edb/ref 1065 3880 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121061 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1c99dfd1-b583-5247-9ac5-c2ada47c0ee9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121061 #edb/ref 1067 "Multiplying a Matrix By a Scalar" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409657 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186409692 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409657 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186409693 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409657 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186409694 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409657 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186409695 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409657 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186409696 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409657 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409657 #edb/ref 1045 "Row and Column Operations on Determinants" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409657 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186409694 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409657 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409657 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409657 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "26bcedbd-f502-5256-b322-6d751a0c036d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409658 #edb/ref 1080 "-256" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409658 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409658 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "08098a6b-f06b-51d7-be19-39ddeb566f21" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409659 #edb/ref 1080 "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409659 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409659 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3f5903e6-2dfb-5988-91aa-d76353ccd959" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409660 #edb/ref 1080 "-7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409660 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409660 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "403dcee3-3eac-5cf3-a329-0558750fb3b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409661 #edb/ref 1080 "-24" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409661 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409661 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4da115ec-321e-53f5-9c9a-06b3818508a4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409662 #edb/ref 1080 "6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409662 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409662 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4e01723b-cc89-5e3f-b933-6517b2094770" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186